//? Title: CIFAR Binary Loading: Bytes, Channels and Labels
//? Subtitle: Check record boundaries before converting planar bytes to NHWC
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Image bytes and labels must stay aligned when records are concatenated, decoded and normalised.
We decode generated CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 records with independently known channel values,
inspect the planar-to-NHWC mapping and reject damaged records. No dataset download or classifier
is needed to establish the binary format contract.
)
\toc

> The method

CIFAR-10 stores one label byte followed by 1024 red, 1024 green and 1024 blue bytes.
CIFAR-100 adds a coarse label before the fine label, giving record widths 3073 and 3074.
For record r, pixel p and channel c, decoding is the permutation

$$
x_{r,\lfloor p/32\rfloor,p\bmod32,c}=b_{rR+q+1024c+p},\qquad \widetilde x=x/255.
$$
\alttext(Each planar channel byte moves to its corresponding interleaved NHWC coordinate, then divides by 255.)

Here q is the number of label bytes and R=3072+q. Validate exact multiples of R and label
ranges before allocating. The raw parser returns Int32 pixels in 0..255 and Int32 labels;
the fixed-name load_cifar10/100 convenience loaders instead return Float32 pixels in [0,1]
and Int64 labels. File-list order determines row order. A permutation must be applied to
both images and labels. Runtime and output memory are O(records times 3072).

The concept diagram has a retained TikZ source in `diagrams/cifar_loader_concept.tikz`.
Regeneration requires LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, and dvisvgm.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/cifar_loader_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/cifar_loader_concept.tikz > build/cifar_loader_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/cifar_loader_concept.svg build/cifar_loader_concept.dvi > build/cifar_loader_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/cifar_loader_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
